Redesign the Calculation Center by referring to the existing Calculation
module in the application.

Reuse the existing Calculation module’s application shell, calculation-type
navigation, dashboard structure, shared components, and styling conventions.
Implement the enhanced readiness-checking and execution-monitoring workflow
described below.

Preserve the existing Calculation module unless changes are explicitly
required. Build the enhanced workflow within Calculation Center.

1. APPLICATION LAYOUT AND CALCULATION NAVIGATION

Keep the existing top header, Citi logo, application name, and primary
application sidebar.

Highlight “Calculation Center” when users enter this module.

Refer to the existing Calculation module’s secondary navigation to display
the available calculation types:
- Net Income Tax
- OCI Tax
- ALLL / PULC
- TCE
- COC
- Tax ETR

Use existing calculation metadata and identifiers where available.
Do not introduce new identifiers or rename existing calculation types
without checking the current implementation.

Selecting a calculation type should:
- Highlight the selected calculation.
- Open its dashboard within the main content area.
- Update the page title, description, and breadcrumb.
- Keep the calculation-type navigation visible.

Use a light-blue background, blue border, and blue text for the selected
calculation type.

For Net Income Tax, show:
- Title: “Net Income Tax”
- Description: “Check readiness, run calculations, and monitor progress.”
- Breadcrumb: Home / Calculation Center / Net Income Tax

2. CALCULATION CONTEXT

At the top of the dashboard, create a “Calculation Context” card containing:
- Sector dropdown.
- Scenario dropdown displaying the scenario name.
- “Check Readiness” button.
- Readiness status badge.
- Last readiness check timestamp.
- “Run Calculation” button.

Reuse the existing sector and scenario data sources, dependencies, and
selection logic where applicable.

Initial state:
- Sector and scenario are not selected.
- Readiness status is “Not Checked”.
- Disable “Check Readiness” until the required selections are provided.
- Disable “Run Calculation”.
- Show:
  “Select a sector and scenario to check calculation readiness.”

When the calculation type, sector, or scenario changes:
- Reset readiness to “Not Checked”.
- Clear readiness messages belonging to the previous selection.
- Do not reuse a successful readiness result from another selection.
- Load execution information relevant to the selected context.

3. READINESS CHECK

When “Check Readiness” is clicked:
- Check readiness for the selected calculation, sector, and scenario.
- Show “Checking Readiness” with a loading indicator.
- Prevent duplicate requests.
- Keep “Run Calculation” disabled while checking.

If readiness passes:
- Show a green “Ready to Calculate” badge.
- Display:
  “All required configurations and inputs are available.”
- Show the readiness check timestamp.
- Enable “Run Calculation” only when readiness has passed for the current
  selection and no calculation is already running for that context.

If readiness fails:
- Show a red “Not Ready” badge.
- Keep “Run Calculation” disabled.
- Automatically expand a “Readiness Issues” panel immediately below
  Calculation Context.
- Explain which prerequisites failed and what is missing or incorrectly
  configured.

Use these columns:
Check | Failure Reason | Suggested Action

Illustrative issue types:
- Tax rate configuration | Missing for selected sector | Configure tax rates
- Scenario inputs | Required forecast data not loaded | Review feeds
- Sector mapping | Incomplete entity mapping | Review mapping

Populate this panel with actual backend-provided issues.
Do not hardcode these examples as real failures.

Provide “Check Again” so users can recheck readiness after fixing issues.
Do not show readiness failure only as a temporary toast.

4. CALCULATION EXECUTION

When the user clicks “Run Calculation”:
- Trigger execution for the selected calculation, sector, and scenario.
- Display the run ID, start time, and overall execution status.
- Prevent duplicate execution.
- Change the action button to disabled “Calculation Running”.
- Lock context controls during the active execution so the displayed
  selection stays consistent with the running job.

Display a horizontal four-stage stepper:
1. Validation
2. Calculation
3. Reconciliation
4. Complete

Readiness is a prerequisite before execution.
Do not include readiness as one of the numbered execution stages.

Use these stage states:
- Pending
- Running
- Completed
- Failed

Stage appearance:
- Completed: green circle with a checkmark.
- Running: blue highlighted circle with a loading indicator.
- Pending: gray numbered circle.
- Failed: red circle with a failure icon.

Update the stepper using actual backend execution status.
Do not simulate stage progression with timers.

5. LIVE EXECUTION DETAILS

Below the stepper, display details for the currently running stage.

Example heading:
“Stage 2: Calculation — Running”

Show:
- Current activity.
- Tasks or substeps within the stage.
- Status of each task.
- Stage start time.
- Elapsed time.
- Last successful status update timestamp.
- Progress percentage only when available from the backend.

Illustrative calculation tasks:
- Load validated inputs — Completed
- Apply tax rates — Completed
- Calculate sector tax expense — Running
- Persist calculation results — Pending

Highlight the currently running task with:
- Light-blue background.
- Blue status icon or spinner.
- Clearly visible “Running” label.

Allow users to select completed stages to inspect their details.
Keep the active execution stage clearly identified even when users inspect
another stage.

Use backend-supported live updates.
If push updates are unavailable, use configurable polling.
Stop polling when execution reaches a terminal status.

If a stage fails:
- Mark that stage as failed.
- Automatically expand the failure details.
- Show the failed task and actual failure reason.
- Show corrective guidance when provided by the backend.
- Keep subsequent stages unexecuted.
- Do not show successful completion.

If status refreshing fails:
- Preserve the last known execution status.
- Display a refresh warning and last successful update timestamp.
- Do not mark the calculation itself as failed solely because a status
  request failed.

6. OUTPUTS AND EXECUTION HISTORY

Below the execution panel, display:

Outputs:
- Before successful completion:
  “Results will be available after successful completion.”
- After successful completion:
  Show available results and supported output actions.

Execution History:
- Show runs relevant to the selected calculation context.
- Include:
  Run ID
  Scenario
  Status
  Started
  Duration
- Selecting a historical run should display its execution details without
  triggering a new calculation.

Reuse existing result and execution-history integrations where available.

